  "body": "Accept the complete effective criterion for a specified onto block self-map of a nonempty two-sided irreducible sofic shift to extend into that shift from a containing SFT. This includes Boyle's Problem 16.3 for mixing shifts and covers all local-rule completions, extension radii and larger containing alphabets. The proof uses the published Place\u2013van Rooijen\u2013Zeitoun (2014) locally testable separation bound; Dykstra's earlier question/steadiness terminology, Barth\u2013Dykstra, and Ballier's normalization and restricted results are credited.\n\nPreprint: **An effective criterion for prescribed block-code extensions**, Alec Kriebel, v1.0, 16 October 2026.\nDOI: https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.23416806\nThe record contains the four-page PDF and source/verification archive. Both public downloads reproduce the reviewed hashes; exact metadata was verified. Tracker: `'Math Puzzles'!A125:D125`.\n\nTwo independent mathematical approach families, two independent bounded priority audits and a peer challenge, root reproductions, and two fresh whole-package adversarial reviews completed. The first whole review's nonregular-input integrity-check hang was repaired globally; the second found zero actionable issues. Original 48 candidate files and the original 3/5 proof-attempt budget are preserved. Acceptance binds reviewed head `4a38c3ccc4cac918fb75fd4b97449f3ebd2e0f7f`.\n\nThe result does not settle existence over all maps, cellular-automaton stability, or the unrestricted reducible-sofic question. The general separation theorem is credited prior machinery. Bounded primary-source reviews established no full predecessor in inspected statements; originality, firstness and present worldwide openness remain unestablished, with inaccessible/version/indexing gaps disclosed. AI tools were used extensively. This is an unrefereed preprint without external human peer review or formal proof certification.\n\nAudit, exact claims, source records, review packets and publication receipts: [PR781 acceptance package](https://github.com/AlecKriebel/Math/tree/main/draft_pr_claimed_solved_700_800_20261012/audits/pr781_4600024).\n",
